  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Generally dry weather through next Sunday. \nDrought conditions will worsen.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure this morning over New England will drift over the\nGulf of Maine waters today. On the backside a weak return flow\ndevelops out of the SW at 5-10mph. A warm front pushing through\nthe Eastern Great Lakes will increase high clouds this afternoon\nas moisture advects in aloft. The boundary layer today remains\nvery dry and with light winds will need to watch some fire \nweather concerns with dry fine fuels. By Thursday, the ridge \nwill begin to shift eastward, allowing for stronger return flow\nout of the SW to advect warmer air into the region. Highs on \nThursday will rebound, in fact soar above average for this time \nof the year, lifting into the lower 60s throughout the forecast \narea.\n\nAnother cold front will cross the forecast area early Friday\nmorning, and will again be mostly moisture starved. There is a\nchance for scattered showers along this boundary, however\nrainfall amounts are likely to remain below a tenth of an inch,\nwith no improvement to drought conditions.\n\nOtherwise, the dry conditons is bad news for the ongoing water\nresources drought impacting the state. Much of Northern Maine, \nCentral Highlands and Eastern Downeast Maine are experiencing \nonly 50-75% of normal precipitation over the last 30 days. While\nin the last 14 days areawide is less than 90% of normal. \nOverall, 30 day Standardized Precipitation Index blend has the \nNorthern 2/3rd of the CWA in the Moderate to Severe Dryness \ncategory with portions of the Upper St. John Basin now dipping \ninto the Extreme Dryness category. \n\nThere is 38 USGS streamflow gages across the CWA which remain \ndismal, with 26 out of the 38 ranked Much Below Normal (<10th \npercentile). USGS Groundwater sites continue to show struggling \ngroundwater conditions, Clayton Lake is at Record Low (deep) \nconditions on record (35 year POR). Millinocket, Kenduskeag, \nHadley Lakes and Calais all reporting Below to Much Below Normal\nconditions. Fort Kent is very low end of Normal conditions \napproaching slipping down into Below Normal conditions. \nTherefore, the US Drought Monitor continues the entire CWA in at\nleast Moderate Drought with 58% of the CWA in Severe Drought, \nthis has the potential to worsen with continued precipitation \ndeficits.",
